In the framework of relativistic cosmology, the standard hot Big Bang model is known to require an early phase of accelerated expansion (an "inflation-like" epoch) in order to resolve the horizon, flatness, and primordial-perturbation problems and to seed the observed structure of the universe. Identify and characterize, within a fundamental physical theory, a concrete dynamical mechanism that:

(i) produces a finite epoch of quasi–de Sitter (or otherwise sufficiently accelerated) expansion of the spatial metric in the very early universe;
(ii) terminates gracefully into a radiation-dominated Friedmann–Lemaître–Robertson–Walker phase compatible with Big Bang nucleosynthesis;
(iii) generates a nearly scale-invariant, adiabatic, Gaussian spectrum of curvature perturbations consistent with present cosmic microwave background observations (e.g., scalar spectral index near 0.965 and tensor-to-scalar ratio bounded by current limits);
(iv) is derived from, rather than postulated within, the fundamental theory (i.e., the inflaton field, its potential, and its initial conditions arise from first principles of the underlying theory rather than being introduced by hand).

State precisely what such a mechanism is, and prove that it satisfies (i)–(iv).

